    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "Figure 2.5. Verification of the existence of a minimum in the normalized density for impure CO 2 , hence in storage capacity, using literature data given in Table 2.1."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 59,
    "level" : "Doctitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 88.667, 304.333, 380.667, 320.667 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman,Bold",
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "2.2.4 A Maximum of Storage Capacity for CO 2 with SO 2  "
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 60,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 87.333, 102.333, 524.667, 295.0 ],
    "font" : "TimesNewRoman",
    "font size" : 12.0,
    "text color" : "[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]",
    "content" : "For impurities which have higher critical temperature than that of CO 2 , no storage capacity minimum would occur in the corresponding CO 2 mixture. Rather, a maximum can appear based on an analysis similar to that for the non-condensable impurities. This has been verified with calculated results for the CO 2   mixture with 2.9 vol % SO 2 (the Cansolv ® absorption system case presented in the previous IEA GHG report PH4/32). As shown in Figure 2.6, a maximum is seen at about 11 MPa, where the storage capacity is increased by over 5%. It should be noted again that the storage capacity is based on pure CO 2 (see discussion for Equation 2.5). It is, therefore, interesting to see that in this peak region, the CO 2 -based capacity is above that of pure CO 2 . In other words, SO 2 does not take any but creates space for CO 2 . This can be rationalized from the consideration that SO 2 decreases average distance between the molecules of the mixture – an opposite effect of that of the non-condensable gases. This can be taken as one positive effect of the presence of SO 2 on CO 2 storage. However, SO 2 causes more concern with regard to its chemical effects on formation rocks. This will be discussed later."
  } ]
